  %% Target design: docs/reference/tasks.md#complete-and-cleanup. Orchestrator 2026-11-08: keeping approved work is a precondition of completion, not a step of it. Orchestrator 2026-11-14: a task copy goes through its normal instance.destroy, as on cancel. 2026-11-15: the task VMs and their release are removed (docs/reference/old-paths.md).
  %% status: draft
  %% command: task:complete
  %% kind: tasks.complete
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant GH as GitHub<br/>repository · PRs
  CLI->>GW: task:complete 42
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit task:complete 42 --yes`
  %%> - syntax: `[task] [--yes] [--json]`
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the group is `settling` or `waiting_for_review`, or `running` or `reviewing` with a watched pull request that merged or closed; a `completed` group retries its removal
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Gateway
  GW->>GH: Compare the task branch<br/>approved...task-42
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - resolves the group's latest approved commit: the latest commit Orbit reviewed (ADR 0203), or else the commit of the latest approval
  %%> - reads origin's task branch head and compares it with that commit, with a `contents: read` token
  %%> - only when GitHub answers that the branch does not exist (404) and the group's pull request merged does that pull request's head stand in; the pull request is read with a `pull_requests: read` token
  %%> - skipped when the group has no approval, or holds no workspace
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the destroy deletes the copy, and a commit that only the copy holds would go with it
  GH-->>GW: Branch reaches it
  %%> Result:
  %%> - otherwise completion refuses with HTTP 409 `tasks.approved_work_unpublished`, names the commit and the branch, and changes nothing: run `task:publish` first
  %%> - every other outcome refuses the same way: a GitHub read that fails, a branch that is gone without a merged pull request
  Note over GW: Complete the group<br/>stop work · subtasks cancelled
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - stores how the watched pull request ended, stops the running implementer, reviewer and checks, and marks the group `completed`
  %%> - a group without a workspace ends here
  %%> - a group that still holds a workspace that is no task copy (a task VM workspace) is `completed` too, and asks for `removal_failure` assistance: task VMs and their release are removed, so nothing removes that workspace
  alt the task owns a task copy
  Note over GW: Admit the copy's destroy<br/>instance.destroy · force
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - no other Operation holds the copy; an open destroy of it is reused, so a repeated complete admits no second one
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - admits the normal `instance.destroy` of the copy with force, the destroy that `task:cancel` admits
  %%> Why:
  %%> - origin already has the approved work, which the compare above proved, so complete needs no `--discard`: what else the copy holds goes with it
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the group is `completed` at once and still names the copy; when the destroy succeeds the group lets go of it and stays `completed`
  %%> - when the destroy fails, the group stays `completed` and keeps the copy; a later complete admits the destroy again
  end
